description:
{
  "name": "Continuous-Reward Thompson Bandit (CR-TED)",
  "algorithm": "Step 1: For each restart, calculate a 'Trail Signature' using a 64-bit XOR-sum of the decision levels of the first 32 variables assigned. Step 2: Define the reward $R = (LBD_{global} / LBD_{current}) \times (1 - \\text{Overlap})$, where $\\text{Overlap}$ is the Jaccard similarity between the current Trail Signature and a circular buffer of the last 8 signatures. Step 3: Integrate this reward into a Multi-Armed Bandit using Thompson Sampling, where each arm (Luby vs. Glucose) maintains a Beta distribution $(\\alpha, \\beta)$. Instead of a binary update, perform a continuous update on the active arm: increment $\\alpha$ by $R$ and $\\beta$ by $1/R$. This allows the bandit to weight the 'quality' of the restart strategy proportionally to the LBD reduction and search diversity achieved. Step 4: Every $2^{10}$ conflicts, scale $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ by 0.5 to ensure the bandit favors recent search topology over historical performance, allowing the solver to adapt to different formula phases (e.g., from search to intensification).",
  "reason": "The original Step 3 used a binary threshold ($R > 1.0$) to update the Beta distribution, which discards critical information about the magnitude of the search improvement. In SAT Competition benchmarks, PAR2 scores are heavily influenced by 'lucky' restarts that find high-quality lemmas (low LBD) in unexplored regions of the search space. By replacing the binary increment with a continuous update ($\alpha += R$, $\beta += 1/R$), the bandit can distinguish between a restart that is marginally better than average and one that provides an exponential improvement in search efficiency. This modification accelerates the convergence of the Thompson Sampling toward the most productive restart strategy for a specific formula phase, reducing the time-to-solution on hard instances."
}

